{"id":"issue:e-evidence-cert-required","type":"Issue","question":"Is a s.106B(4) certificate required before electronic evidence is admitted?"},
{"id":"issue:e-evidence-who-signs","type":"Issue","question":"Who must sign a s.106B certificate?"},
{"id":"issue:e-evidence-late-cert","type":"Issue","question":"Can a s.106B certificate be filed late?"},
{"id":"issue:e-evidence-affidavit-as-cert","type":"Issue","question":"Can an expert affidavit replace a s.106B certificate?"},
{"id":"issue:e-evidence-maker-testifies","type":"Issue","question":"Is a certificate needed where the maker of an electronic document testifies?"},
{"id":"issue:e-evidence-in-scc","type":"Issue","question":"Do strict rules of evidence (incl. s.106B) apply in the Small Claims Court?"},
{"id":"issue:set-aside-default","type":"Issue","question":"When will a default/ex parte judgment be set aside?"},
{"id":"issue:regular-vs-irregular","type":"Issue","question":"Was the default judgment regular or irregular?"},
{"id":"issue:service-whatsapp","type":"Issue","question":"Is service by WhatsApp valid?"},
{"id":"issue:scc-jurisdiction-employment","type":"Issue","question":"Does the SCC have jurisdiction over claims arising from employment?"},
{"id":"issue:adverse-inference","type":"Issue","question":"When may a court draw an adverse inference for failure to call a witness or produce evidence?"},
{"id":"issue:loan-vs-other-arrangement","type":"Issue","question":"Was money advanced a repayable loan or part of another arrangement?"},
{"id":"issue:formal-agreement-needed","type":"Issue","question":"Is a formal written agreement required to prove an arrangement?"},
{"id":"issue:bailee-vicarious-liability","type":"Issue","question":"Is a bailee liable for theft of goods by its own guards/agents?"},
{"id":"issue:interim-measures-arbitration","type":"Issue","question":"What is the test for court-ordered interim measures pending arbitration?"},
{"id":"issue:res-judicata","type":"Issue","question":"Is the matter res judicata?"},
{"id":"issue:reopen-additional-evidence","type":"Issue","question":"When may a party re-open its case to adduce additional evidence?"},
{"id":"issue:insurer-statutory-mgmt","type":"Issue","question":"Does an insurer's statutory management/moratorium bar proceedings?"},
{"id":"pr:cert-mandatory","type":"Principle","text":"Electronic evidence is inadmissible without a s.106B(4) certificate.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:cert-signer","type":"Principle","text":"The certificate must be signed by the person responsible for the device or the relevant activities (e.g. CCTV owner, phone owner), not merely whoever copied it.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:cert-late-allowed","type":"Principle","text":"In the interest of justice a party may be given liberty to file the certificate later.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:cert-late-not-after-trial","type":"Principle","text":"A certificate cannot be produced after the admissibility issue has been determined at trial; its integrity could not then be verified.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:cert-substantive","type":"Principle","text":"The certificate requirement is substantive, not a procedural technicality curable under Art.159(2)(d).","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:affidavit-not-cert","type":"Principle","text":"An expert's affidavit describing the device is not equivalent to a s.106B certificate.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:maker-testifies-cures","type":"Principle","text":"Emails may be admitted without a certificate where their makers testify and the contents are undisputed; a photo of third-party writings still needs a certificate.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:scc-not-strict-evidence","type":"Principle","text":"The Small Claims Court is not bound by strict rules of evidence; it may consider relevant material, and a formal written agreement is not in itself determinative.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:scc-exempt-106b-obiter","type":"Principle","text":"The mandatory certificate rule applies in civil and criminal courts except where statute excludes strict rules of evidence, such as the Small Claims Court.","kind":"obiter"},
{"id":"pr:regular-default-discretion","type":"Principle","text":"An irregular default judgment is set aside as of right; setting aside a regular one is discretionary and needs a satisfactory explanation and a defence raising a triable issue.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:delay-must-be-explained","type":"Principle","text":"Delay in entering appearance must be plausibly explained by those responsible (including the insurer).","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:whatsapp-service-valid","type":"Principle","text":"Service by WhatsApp is proper where the recipient does not deny owning the number and the record links the number to them (e.g. an M-Pesa message bearing their name).","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:scc-no-employment","type":"Principle","text":"Claims founded on an employment relationship are excluded from the SCC by s.13(5), however the relief is framed; the suit is struck out, not dismissed.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:adverse-inference","type":"Principle","text":"A court may infer that evidence a party could have produced (e.g. its own representative) would have been adverse to it.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:bailee-vicarious","type":"Principle","text":"A bailee for reward is liable where its servants or agents steal the goods in its custody.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:s7-supportive","type":"Principle","text":"Court interim measures under s.7 Arbitration Act support the arbitral process; the Giella injunction test is not the governing test.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:res-judicata","type":"Principle","text":"A matter finally determined between the same parties by a competent court cannot be reopened via judicial review.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:reopen-explain-delay","type":"Principle","text":"A party seeking to re-open its case for additional evidence must explain why it was not produced earlier, and the evidence must align with the pleadings.","kind":"ratio"},
{"id":"pr:statutory-mgmt-no-bar","type":"Principle","text":"An insurer under statutory management remains a legal person able to sue and be sued; a moratorium does not invalidate a judgment against its insured.","kind":"ratio"},
